vcl: a missing CJK font keeps its kind, and says what drew it

A document names a Chinese font the system lacks (微软雅黑, 等线,
思源黑体 on a Mac; 等线 on an English Windows). The name has no
substitution entry, so the lookup scored every family by attributes.
A family whose name holds Han characters counts as a Chinese text
face, and on a Mac in Chinese every family is named in Chinese, so
Kaiti, Xingkai, Libian, Wawati and Yuppy tie with PingFang; the tie
goes to hash order. The same file drew STKaiti TC in Writer and
Yuppy TC in Impress. The language test also takes any CJK language
for any other: Windows drew 等线 in MS UI Gothic, a Japanese face
(Ely R20.38).

A missing name in a CJK run, or a CJK name, now takes the run
language's default list (VCL.xcu DefaultFonts) before the shared
substitution lists: CJK_TEXT for Song, Ming, Kai and Fangsong names,
CJK_DISPLAY (sans) for the rest, as Word and PowerPoint fall back to
SimSun and Microsoft YaHei. The shared lists come after: their tails
cross the kind (宋体 drew Arial Unicode MS on a Mac). List members
are found by any of their names, as a Mac in Chinese keeps PingFang
as 苹方-简.

On Linux fontconfig answers first, and it adds a generic family only
when the document gives a family type; Word gives 宋体 "auto", so
宋体, 思源宋体, 仿宋 and 楷体 drew in Noto Sans CJK. A Song-kind name
in a CJK run now adds "serif" there. One rule names the kind for
both: IsTextCJKFontName in unotools.

Every substitution is logged once per name, in the ely.vcl.fonts
area: the name asked, the family taken, and why; every glyph
fallback once per asked font and family taken.

